    The spinel binary answers a public response from Thruster's cache
    
    A Rails app deployed from its own Dockerfile runs Thruster in front of
    Puma, and Thruster answers every `Cache-Control: public` response with a
    positive max-age from memory after the first. The rails lane on the
    campfire bench has Thruster, and the CRuby lanes carry its rules as
    ThrusterCache middleware; the spinel binary had neither, so its avatar
    cell ran the whole controller (before_actions, the CSP policy's 89
    URI.joins, the SVG render) for a response every other lane answers from
    memory: 5.6k req/s against ruby's 76k.
    
    Tep::ThrusterCache holds the same rules for the binary, which has no
    Rack, and Tep::App#dispatch consults it before leasing a database
    connection, as Thruster answers before Puma. A file the app sends is
    stored as its path, so a hit is the same sendfile without the app.
    
    Measured on rubix2 (spinel 1f02852df), campfire at the pinned 11d3fb9:
    the avatar route 5,952 -> 70,535 req/s, body byte-identical, X-Cache
    hit, Set-Cookie dropped as Thruster drops it. /rooms/1 unchanged. /up,
    which is never stored, pays the lookup a miss pays: 39.5k -> 37.5k
    interleaved over three runs, as it does behind Thruster.
    ROUNDHOUSE_RESPONSE_CACHE=0 turns it off, as for the CRuby lanes.
    
    tests/tep_thruster_cache.rs holds each rule (25 checks) through the real
    threaded server over the scripted socket; the spinel toolchain suite
    passes locally (62/62).
    
    Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5.5 <[redacted-email]>

